1
00:00:06,570 --> 00:00:17,290
Mia's phone records the battery level every hour from 8 am to 2 pm: 100, 88, 75, 66, 52, 40 and 31 percent.

2
00:00:17,340 --> 00:00:20,480
Is the battery running down roughly steadily?

3
00:00:20,530 --> 00:00:23,790
Pause the video and choose.

4
00:00:23,840 --> 00:00:24,090
Yes.

5
00:00:24,140 --> 00:00:31,400
It falls by about 11 to 13 percent every hour, so the points lie close to a straight line.

6
00:00:31,450 --> 00:00:36,790
Remember: for a line graph, plot the points, then join them with straight lines.

7
00:00:36,840 --> 00:00:39,570
Time goes on the horizontal axis.

8
00:00:39,620 --> 00:00:47,950
A time series graph shows a quantity measured at regular time intervals, with the points joined by straight lines.

9
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:51,450
It shows the trend: the general direction of change.

10
00:00:51,500 --> 00:00:53,350
Up, down, or steady.

11
00:00:53,400 --> 00:00:54,050
Your turn.

12
00:00:54,100 --> 00:00:56,000
What is the battery's trend?

13
00:00:56,050 --> 00:00:59,280
Pause the video and choose.

14
00:00:59,330 --> 00:01:01,130
It is a downward trend.

15
00:01:01,180 --> 00:01:05,310
Points between the plotted times are only estimates.

16
00:01:05,360 --> 00:01:12,220
At 10:30, the battery was probably about 70 percent, halfway between 75 and 66.

17
00:01:12,270 --> 00:01:17,000
Continuing the trend, the battery would reach 0 at about 4 or 5 pm.

18
00:01:17,050 --> 00:01:21,400
But predictions far into the future are less reliable.

19
00:01:21,450 --> 00:01:22,760
Here is the method.

20
00:01:22,810 --> 00:01:27,440
Step 1: put time on the horizontal axis, with equal steps.

21
00:01:27,490 --> 00:01:31,650
Step 2: choose a vertical scale that fits the data.

22
00:01:31,700 --> 00:01:37,200
Step 3: plot each point accurately, and join them with straight lines.

23
00:01:37,250 --> 00:01:41,190
Step 4: describe the trend, and any big changes.

24
00:01:41,240 --> 00:01:44,820
In which hour did the battery drop most?

25
00:01:44,870 --> 00:01:48,270
The drops are 12, 13, 9, 14, 12 and 9.

26
00:01:48,320 --> 00:01:52,540
The biggest is 14, from 11 am to 12 noon.

27
00:01:52,590 --> 00:01:53,240
Your turn.

28
00:01:53,290 --> 00:01:56,000
How much did it drop from 1 pm to 2 pm?

29
00:01:56,050 --> 00:01:59,240
Pause and check the table.

30
00:01:59,290 --> 00:02:04,350
From 40 percent to 31 percent is a drop of 9 percent.

31
00:02:04,400 --> 00:02:06,030
Be careful with scales.

32
00:02:06,080 --> 00:02:11,900
If the vertical axis starts at 60 percent instead of 0, small changes look huge.

33
00:02:11,950 --> 00:02:15,010
Always check where the axis starts.

34
00:02:15,060 --> 00:02:16,970
Sam draws the battery graph.

35
00:02:17,020 --> 00:02:18,920
Which line is wrong?

36
00:02:18,970 --> 00:02:20,040
Line 1 is wrong.

37
00:02:20,090 --> 00:02:25,040
In a time series graph, time always goes on the horizontal axis.

38
00:02:25,090 --> 00:02:28,100
Time across, battery percentage up.

39
00:02:28,150 --> 00:02:35,780
Scientists use time series graphs to show how the world's average temperature has changed since the 1800s.

40
00:02:35,830 --> 00:02:41,980
The graph goes up and down from year to year, but the overall trend is upward.

41
00:02:42,030 --> 00:02:43,230
One last question.

42
00:02:43,280 --> 00:02:47,460
Is a prediction 3 days ahead more reliable, or less reliable?

43
00:02:47,510 --> 00:02:50,730
Pause the video and choose.

44
00:02:50,780 --> 00:02:51,700
Less reliable.

45
00:02:51,750 --> 00:02:56,240
The further ahead you predict, the more things can change.

46
00:02:56,290 --> 00:03:01,020
To remember: regular time intervals go on the horizontal axis.

47
00:03:01,070 --> 00:03:04,010
Join the points with straight lines.

48
00:03:04,060 --> 00:03:06,370
Describe the trend.

49
00:03:06,420 --> 00:03:10,790
Values in between, and in the future, are only estimates.

50
00:03:10,840 --> 00:03:16,270
Great work! Now practise lesson 16.2 on Camday.
